{"reason": "The initial plan aims to replace CLS pooling with attention-weighted mean pooling to improve DeBERTa representation quality. Historical successful experiments show that mean pooling with a simple transformer + LightGBM stacking works well, while in-head fusion of handcrafted features failed. Therefore, I refine the plan to use mean pooling (simpler and proven) instead of attention-weighted pooling, and keep handcrafted features only in the XGBoost meta-classifier rather than fusing into the transformer head. This avoids overcomplexity and leverages successful stacking strategies. The DeBERTa validation log loss (0.3846) and ensemble log loss (0.2797) indicate room for improvement; mean pooling can capture distributed stylistic signals better than CLS pooling. The module modifications are model_design (change pooling in the classifier head) and training_evaluation (retain embeddings for XGBoost stacking without changes). Data processing remains adequate.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current CLS token pooling in the DeBERTa model with mean pooling of all token embeddings weighted by attention mask. In the forward method, compute `last_hidden_state * attention_mask.unsqueeze(-1)`, sum over tokens, and divide by `attention_mask.sum(-1, keepdim=True)` to obtain pooled embeddings. Keep the classifier head unchanged (1024->128->3). Do NOT add handcrafted feature fusion into the transformer head, as historical failed experiments show it harms performance. This change leverages mean pooling's ability to capture distributed stylistic signals, addressing the initial plan's concern about CLS pooling ignoring non-CLS tokens. The implementation preserves checkpoint compatibility by modifying only the classification head.", "training_evaluation": "Retain the existing training loop and evaluation procedure unchanged, as historical successful experiments indicate that a simple transformer with stacked classifiers (XGBoost) is effective. Continue to extract DeBERTa embeddings (now mean pooled) for the XGBoost meta-classifier, which uses handcrafted features concatenated with embeddings. Do not alter training hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, optimizer, scheduler, early stopping) or ensemble weighting. The mean pooling improvement should lead to better embeddings, thus improving XGBoost performance and potentially increasing the DeBERTa weight in the ensemble."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan aims to replace CLS pooling with attention-weighted mean pooling to improve DeBERTa representation quality. Historical successful experiments show that mean pooling with a simple transformer + LightGBM stacking works well, while in-head fusion of handcrafted features failed. Therefore, I refine the plan to use mean pooling (simpler and proven) instead of attention-weighted pooling, and keep handcrafted features only in the XGBoost meta-classifier rather than fusing into the transformer head. This avoids overcomplexity and leverages successful stacking strategies. The DeBERTa validation log loss (0.3846) and ensemble log loss (0.2797) indicate room for improvement; mean pooling can capture distributed stylistic signals better than CLS pooling. The module modifications are model_design (change pooling in the classifier head) and training_evaluation (retain embeddings for XGBoost stacking without changes). Data processing remains adequate.",
